course: dictionary
lesson: 41
title: Access: a way in, or the right to use
level: primary-5
stars: 1
explain: Access is a noun and a verb. As a noun, it means a way of getting into a place, or the right or chance to use or see something. «Wheelchair access» means a way in that a wheelchair user can get through. As a verb, to access something means to reach it or use it, especially information on a computer.
explain: Read the words around access to find its meaning. «No access» on a sign tells you that you cannot go in. «Emergency access must be kept clear» tells you it is a way that must not be blocked, so people can get through quickly.
explain: Try the swap test. In «to access the internet», put in «to use the internet». It still makes sense, so access means reach or use here. In «a password to access», the password lets you get in, so access means get in.
fact: Access means a way of getting into a place, or the right or chance to use or see something.
fact: To access a file on a computer means to open it and get into it.
fact: «Have access to» means you are allowed, or able, to use something.
fact: «Disabled people campaigned for access», meaning they worked hard for ways in that everyone can use, such as ramps.
fact: The US passed the Americans with Disabilities Act in 1990, and the UN agreed a convention on disabled people's rights in 2006.
exercise: «The sign said no access, so we turned back.» What does no access mean? | Everyone must run | You may go in if you wave | *You cannot go in | The gate is painted blue
exercise: «To access a file on a computer» means what? | Throw it away | Make it louder | *Open it and get into it | Print it on paper only
exercise: «Ramps give wheelchair users access to the shop.» What does access mean here? | A kind of food | A loud noise | *A way of getting into a place | A type of shoe
exercise: «Emergency access must be kept clear.» Why must it be kept clear? | So it can be painted | *So people can get through quickly | So it can be sold | So it can grow
test: «Ella needed a password to access the school website.» What does access mean here? | Forget | Ignore | Build | *Get into and use
test: Which sentence uses access as a noun? | *The ramp gave wheelchair users access to the shop | We will access the shop later today | She will access her email at noon | They access the park every day
exam: «Students have access to the library.» What does have access to mean? | Are afraid of it | Have lost it | Have painted it | *Are allowed to use it
